#%PAM-1.0
# Password OR phone approval for sudo. Rationale in docs/phone-auth.md.
#
# pam_unix is FIRST and `sufficient`: a correct password succeeds outright and
# pam_duo never runs, so routine sudo does not wake the phone. Press Enter on
# an empty password prompt to fall through to a Duo push instead. That ordering
# also keeps the push-fatigue surface at zero for normal use - a push only ever
# arrives because someone deliberately declined to type the password.
#
# DANGER, verified empirically: pam_duo's default failmode is "safe", which
# means FAIL-OPEN. duo_unix ships /etc/duo/pam_duo.conf with EMPTY keys and
# failmode=safe, and a MISSING conf file also fails open. With pam_duo marked
# `sufficient`, that state authenticates ANY password from ANY user. Verified
# 2026-07-30 with pamtester run as root (matching setuid sudo): failmode=safe
# ACCEPTED a deliberately wrong password; failmode=secure rejected it.
#
# failmode cannot be forced from here: pam_duo accepts only `conf=` as a module
# argument (man 8 pam_duo). Safety therefore depends on the conf file, which
# cannot live in this public repo because it holds the secret key. So:
#   - `conf=` is stated explicitly below rather than left implicit.
#   - scripts/harden-auth.sh writes that file with failmode=secure ALWAYS, even
#     when no Duo keys are supplied, so the unconfigured state fails CLOSED.
#   - that script refuses to remove NOPASSWD until pamtester proves a wrong
#     password is rejected.
# If you ever hand-edit /etc/duo/pam_duo.conf, keep failmode=secure.
#
# pam_duo is `sufficient` rather than `required` on purpose: this is
# password-OR-phone, not password-AND-phone. To make it true 2FA, change
# pam_unix to `required` and pam_duo to `required`, and drop pam_deny.
#
# pam_faillock is deliberately absent here. `sufficient` on pam_unix returns
# before pam_faillock's `authsucc` could reset the counter, so failed attempts
# would accumulate across weeks until the account locked itself out for no
# reason. Brute-force defence stays on the login path (system-auth), which is
# where an attacker who does not already have a shell actually arrives; by the
# time someone can invoke sudo they are already running as this user.
auth      sufficient   pam_unix.so     try_first_pass
auth      sufficient   pam_duo.so      conf=/etc/duo/pam_duo.conf
auth      required     pam_deny.so

account   include      system-auth
session   include      system-auth
session   optional     pam_systemd.so  class=none
